    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "Use $(1+x)^{2}(1+x)^{8}=(1+x)^{10}$ to prove that ${^{8}C_{4}}+{^{2}C_{1}}{^{8}C_{3}}+{^{8}C_{2}}={^{10}C_{4}}$.",
        "solution_text": "Expand each side using the binomial theorem:\n$\\begin{align*}(1+x)^{2}&={^{2}C_{0}}+{^{2}C_{1}}x+{^{2}C_{2}}x^{2}\\\\(1+x)^{8}&=\\sum_{s=0}^{8}{^{8}C_{s}}x^{s}\\\\(1+x)^{10}&=\\sum_{t=0}^{10}{^{10}C_{t}}x^{t}.\\end{align*}$\nProducts contribute to $x^{4}$ when their powers add to $4$: the pairs are $(0,4)$, $(1,3)$ and $(2,2)$.\nThe coefficient of $x^{4}$ on the left is\n$\\begin{align*}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}{^{2}C_{0}}{^{8}C_{4}}+{^{2}C_{1}}{^{8}C_{3}}+{^{2}C_{2}}{^{8}C_{2}}\\\\&={^{8}C_{4}}+{^{2}C_{1}}{^{8}C_{3}}+{^{8}C_{2}}.\\end{align*}$\nThe coefficient of $x^{4}$ on the right is $^{10}C_{4}$. Equating coefficients gives\n${^{8}C_{4}}+{^{2}C_{1}}{^{8}C_{3}}+{^{8}C_{2}}={^{10}C_{4}}$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "By comparing coefficients of $x^{7}$ in $(1+x)^{7}(1+x)^{7}=(1+x)^{14}$, prove that $\\sum_{r=0}^{7}({^{7}C_{r}})^{2}={^{14}C_{7}}$.",
        "solution_text": "The binomial theorem gives\n$\\begin{align*}(1+x)^{7}(1+x)^{7}&=\\left(\\sum_{r=0}^{7}{^{7}C_{r}}x^{r}\\right)\\left(\\sum_{s=0}^{7}{^{7}C_{s}}x^{s}\\right)\\\\(1+x)^{14}&=\\sum_{t=0}^{14}{^{14}C_{t}}x^{t}.\\end{align*}$\nOn the left, a product contributes to $x^{7}$ when $r+s=7$. For every $r$ from $0$ to $7$, the matching index is $s=7-r$.\nUsing symmetry $^{7}C_{7-r}={^{7}C_{r}}$, the coefficient on the left is\n$\\begin{align*}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\sum_{r=0}^{7}{^{7}C_{r}}{^{7}C_{7-r}}\\\\&=\\sum_{r=0}^{7}({^{7}C_{r}})^{2}.\\end{align*}$\nThe coefficient of $x^{7}$ on the right is $^{14}C_{7}$. Equating coefficients proves\n$\\sum_{r=0}^{7}({^{7}C_{r}})^{2}={^{14}C_{7}}$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Use $\\left(x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{5}\\left(x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{5}=\\left(x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{10}$ to prove that $\\sum_{r=0}^{5}({^{5}C_{r}})^{2}={^{10}C_{5}}$ by equating constant terms, where $x\\ne0$.",
        "solution_text": "Expand each side using the binomial theorem:\n$\\begin{align*}\\left(x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{5}\\left(x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{5}&=\\left(\\sum_{r=0}^{5}{^{5}C_{r}}x^{5-2r}\\right)\\left(\\sum_{s=0}^{5}{^{5}C_{s}}x^{5-2s}\\right)\\\\\\left(x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{10}&=\\sum_{t=0}^{10}{^{10}C_{t}}x^{10-2t}.\\end{align*}$\nOn the right, a term is constant when\n$\\begin{align*}10-2t&=0\\\\t&=5.\\end{align*}$\nThe constant term on the right is $^{10}C_{5}$.\nOn the left, the powers in a product must sum to zero:\n$\\begin{align*}(5-2r)+(5-2s)&=0\\\\r+s&=5.\\end{align*}$\nThus $s=5-r$, with both indices between $0$ and $5$. Using symmetry $^{5}C_{5-r}={^{5}C_{r}}$, the constant term on the left is\n$\\begin{align*}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\sum_{r=0}^{5}{^{5}C_{r}}{^{5}C_{5-r}}\\\\&=\\sum_{r=0}^{5}({^{5}C_{r}})^{2}.\\end{align*}$\nEquating constant terms proves\n$\\sum_{r=0}^{5}({^{5}C_{r}})^{2}={^{10}C_{5}}$."
      }
    ],
